00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.309 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve here again  i want to talk a bit about pronunciation  because it was a subject on a forum at  link lingq.com which is our language  learning community  and someone had posted a video of an  interview with a lady who had a company  that did accent reduction  and helped people  work on their english  and  on their english pronunciation because  it is a fact that uh in an  english-speaking country if you have a  strong  accent  that can be a disadvantage in the  workplace  so i'm going to talk a little bit about  what i consider to be the importance of  accent reduction and how we go about  doing it  first of all i think that in most  situations  it is far more important to speak well  to have a  broad vocabulary to have natural  phrasing  is much more important than the accent  unless you want to be a tv announcer or  something i know lots of people in  canada who are very competent who  communicate extremely well who give  presentations  and who use the language well but have  accents not necessarily a problem  normally people with poor act or unless  of course you're difficult to understand  and that's where sometimes people from  say south asia india  you know they're so  convinced they speak english so well and  they speak at rapid fire and it's  actually difficult to understand them  so as long as you're understandable  the question of accent reduction  is really a false problem  it's more a matter of the ability to  communicate effectively  so  accent reduction becomes more a matter  of vanity  than anything else so whether you are a  recent immigrant to canada wanting to  improve your accent in english or  whether you're learning a foreign  language we would all like to sound more  like the native speaker even though we  know we can't achieve it and there's  nothing wrong with that because after  all the native speaker is the model  of how the language is spoken so if we  model ourselves on the native speaker  when it comes to the phrasing and the  you know usage and so forth and so on  of course we would like to also model  ourselves  on the pronunciation of the native  speaker  there are those politically correct you  know sociolinguistic type you know  cultural sensitivity type people who  want to say well if you're hispanic then  you should keep your spanish accent  because that's  that represents who you are well fine if  that's what you want to do  uh but to me that doesn't make sense  then why don't you also  have you know if you're german or  japanese put all the verbs at the end of  the sentence why don't you imitate other  aspects or at least retain other aspects  of your language when you speak english  to me it doesn't make sense but  realistically most people are not going  to achieve native like pronunciation and  really it's not a problem so i kind of  have this contradictory view i think  it's not so important to sound like a  native  but it should be our goal even though we  know we're not going to achieve it  because it's part of the psychology  to learn to speak a language well i've  always said this its attitude is so  important you you almost think that you  become one of them i become chinese or  japanese or french i want to be like  them i indicate i imitate their gestures  i start thinking a little bit the way  they do so that's natural and the more  you can separate yourself from your own  culture  at least while you're speaking that  other language the better you're going  to do in all aspects of language  learning so why should you just hang on  to your accent as a sign of your  identity it doesn't make sense to me  now  how do we then  get rid of our accent  to me it's number one a matter of will  and i'll use an example when i was a  little child  my father could wiggle his ears and i  couldn't  and i sat by the television we had a  television i remember whenever i sat at  the television i would tell my brain  wiggle your ears and i don't know how  long i did this but i know that one day  if you can see  probably not very visible but i can  wiggle my ears in other words we can  will our brain to do things  so it's extremely important that you  want to  adopt  this other accent  uh so that's number one and you have to  think that you can do it the more  confident you are the more willing you  are like an actor many actors and  actresses are very good at imitating  other accents  because they're used to projecting  themselves into the role of someone else  so as a language learner you have to try  to project yourself into that role i am  now chinese  i'm not an english-speaking canadian  trying to learn chinese i am chinese i  can do it  so that's extremely important  in terms of the activities that we  should undertake i personally am not a  great believer in the international  phonetic  alphabet or  trying to focus on individual sounds and  where to put your tongue and all this  kind of stuff  i think it's difficult to remember those  things when we're speaking i mean there  are a few instructions like the french  i've heard the instruction that you go  ooh  and then try to say ee so if you go  come up with an uh  there's a few like that i mean in  english you stick your tongue between  your teeth to make the or think there's  a few like that but i don't think  there's so many there's not there's not  even 10 per language there's a few  crucial sounds in a language that might  be difficult and might be amenable to  that kind of explanation  similarly with the ipa because in every  language there are so many variations  uh within the language even amongst  people from the same area of the  language so  which of the forms of the native speaker  are you going to try to isolate with  this ipa  i think you're far better off and here's  where we come to that the key activity  is to listen  and you an immigrant to canada from  china in his 20s he'd been in canada for  three or four years his english was  flawless i thought he was born in canada  and what did he do he listened he  listened to a very limited amount of  content like i don't know 15 20 minutes  a thousand times a thousand times now  not many people have the discipline to  do that but that's what he did  i know in my chinese learning there was  some content that i loved i loved it  there were these comic dialogues i just  listened to them over and over and over  again even when i couldn't understand  them but it gave me the rhythm of the  language it worked on my brain and it  helped me in my pronunciation and  particularly it helped me with tones in  chinese so and i also recommend  listening at first to the language read  slowly because it's going to be easier  for the brain to identify the little  sound the difference differences in the  sounds so listen initially to a limited  amount of content read very slowly  and then start listening to the language  read at normal speed  and initially just listen  don't try to record yourself and compare  when you're not very good it's only  going to be  distraction interference and at some  point you start to speak and when you  start to speak you imitate and you and  when you imitate focus on imitating the  intonation  rather than the individual sounds if the  intonation if you can catch that  intonation it's almost like a pitchfork  in music if your brain can pick up that  intonation the music  other things are going to fall into  place  so to summarize  uh yes we obviously want to imitate the  accent the pronunciation of the native  speaker just as we imitate everything  about the native speaker and how he  handles his own language or her own  language number two most people are not  going to achieve native speaker  pronunciation and it doesn't matter as  long as they can be understood it is  more important to speak correctly using  normal usage that's number two number  three to get closer to native speaking  pronunciation you have to want to do it  you have to will it you have to be able  to see yourself project yourself in the  role of a native speaker of that  language and feel confident that you can  do it and fourth  the main activity is lots of listening  to a limited amount of content content  over and over initially read very slowly  give your brain a chance to pick out the  different sounds  and then  fast and i'm talking about a period of  six months maybe two months very slow  and four months faster and when you  start to speak  focus on the intonation and the rhythm  and don't worry so much about the  individual sounds there you have it  that's my take on pronunciation i got it  within the 10 minutes

